  "answer": "To reduce the risk of language models generating hate speech, insults, profanity, and threats, researchers have proposed combining data filtering, output filtering, and steering to minimize toxicity at different stages (https://www.deepmind.com/blog/challenges-in-detoxifying-language-models). A primary intervention is to filter pre-training data using an automated toxicity classifier by removing or down-weighting highly toxic texts during corpus curation (https://www.deepmind.com/blog/challenges-in-detoxifying-language-models). The specific mechanism for this involves evaluating texts with the Perspective API and excluding those that exceed a toxicity score threshold prior to model training or fine-tuning (https://www.deepmind.com/blog/challenges-in-detoxifying-language-models). Additionally, output filtering can be implemented as a mechanism during the decoding phase, where each candidate continuation is scored by a fine-tuned BERT toxicity classifier so that toxic continuations are rejected (https://www.deepmind.com/blog/challenges-in-detoxifying-language-models)."
